WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
I never thought the day would come in which&nbsp;
I would make a full-fledged devoid of logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.780
video for an MCU movie. There would always be some&nbsp;
silly aspects for Marvel movies, but never enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:15.120
to the point of justifying an entire video.&nbsp;
However, I think Antman Quantumania is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.860
the most stupid MCU movies to the point where&nbsp;
the devoid of logic aspects were overflowing.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.780
Within the first 10 minutes of the movie, it&nbsp;
immediately requires a major contrivance for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.360
the entire story to exist. And that contrivance&nbsp;
is Janet Van Dyne not telling anyone about an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.280
extremely dangerous enemy in the quantum realm.&nbsp;
Even when Cassie Lang brings out a Quantum device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.720
that can send a signal down there, she&nbsp;
doesn’t warn them about Kang. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.120
she just acts like an unjustified crazy person and&nbsp;
half haphazardly pulls the power on this device as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:53.340
if it were my mom unplugging my PC at 1am while&nbsp;
I’m playing Minecraft. It’s kind of wild here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.140
that she isn’t open and explains her reasoning.&nbsp;
These are all rational people and they would most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.480
likely listen to Janet’s warnings given that she&nbsp;
lived down there for many years. The writer tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.980
to play it off as Janet not wanting to go into&nbsp;
it because she’s ashamed of the bad things she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:13.620
done and probably thinks she can get away with&nbsp;
not telling them about Kang. Which news alert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.720
you live in a family that’s obsessed with the&nbsp;
Quantum realm and actively wants to explore it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.780
so you should probably give them some warnings.&nbsp;
It’s an inevitable conflict that you can’t ignore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.000
So yeah, it’s obviously this way so that we can&nbsp;
have this malfunction and I honestly feel like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.920
would be better if Cassie Lang made a design flaw&nbsp;
and that’s why they get sucked into the Quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.140
realm. Because when you think about it, Cassie&nbsp;
Lang has to be absurdly smart for being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.480
make this, regardless of having Hank’s journals.&nbsp;
Like her intellect is kind of swept under the rug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.860
and maybe the writer should have explored the&nbsp;
carelessness of Cassie Lang wanting to make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.680
contribution to society, and the mounting pressure&nbsp;
of her family all being heroes. So maybe she cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.660
corners and rushes along this tech so that she&nbsp;
could feel validated in this family that’s hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.940
live up to, given their massive accomplishments.&nbsp;
Personally, I feel like this already gives Cassie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.980
Lang more depth as a character instead of just&nbsp;
being a generic good-doer who basically has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.620
flaws. Besides her getting arrested at a protest,&nbsp;
which really isn’t a flaw. Have her deal with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.940
consequences of sending her loved ones to the&nbsp;
Quantum realm and have her rise to the occasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.580
and save them. That’s a proper hero's journey.&nbsp;
This actually gives her a tangible character arc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.860
and if I were to ask you what her arc for the&nbsp;
movie currently is, you’d probably struggle.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.500
Moving along into the Quantum realm, this entire&nbsp;
setting is extremely confusing and inconsistent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.420
Or it lacks any semblance of logic because the&nbsp;
writer doesn’t make an attempt at explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.980
anything within the quantum realm. Some things&nbsp;
are explained, but there’s just so much random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.380
shit that’s constantly happening in every scene&nbsp;
that it turns the movie into a silly mess. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.960
this wouldn’t really bother me, but the MCU is&nbsp;
pretty fleshed out overall and is based in logic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.520
Like you can generally explain anything that has&nbsp;
happened in the past with complete understanding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.680
So the issue with this movie is that they&nbsp;
go full-blown into wacky territory and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.660
feels jarring for the tone. I just have so many&nbsp;
questions like how are they even breathing here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.580
why are there aliens that look like broccoli,&nbsp;
what is the science behind these floating ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:22.680
anything regarding populations, how they&nbsp;
even live, how they’re making these cities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:28.200
and really I can keep going on but you get it.&nbsp;
It feels like Antman Quantumania turned into Rick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.480
and Morty which isn't a coincidence because the&nbsp;
writer for the movie came from that show. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.760
all the silly and unexplained sy-fy elements in&nbsp;
Rick and Morty are fine because the show never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.260
takes itself that seriously. Which I guess&nbsp;
Quantumania is trying to be more humorful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.980
but it normally falls flat in my&nbsp;
opinion, and is always kind of awkward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.820
In essence, the entire Quantum realm just feels&nbsp;
like an excuse to throw whatever the writer wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.860
on screen, and then for any potential explanation,&nbsp;
they’ll stamp the word quantum in front of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.080
So basically the entire setting for the movie&nbsp;
is devoid of logic, but it is by design.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.480
So getting back to the story, Scott and Cassie&nbsp;
are split up from the Van Dyne dysfunctional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.340
family team and they get attacked by Kang’s army&nbsp;
while they’re with some rebels. What happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.800
here is probably one of the most unappealing fight&nbsp;
sequences visually which is really sad considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.120
that the environment they’re in essentially has&nbsp;
endless possibilities for how it could look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.100
but for some reason, it looks really dull. During&nbsp;
this fight scene, it’s revealed that Cassie has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.560
her own suit, which is immediately confusing&nbsp;
because she’s been in life-threatening scenarios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.960
on multiple occasions earlier on, and for some&nbsp;
reason, she chooses to not activate her suit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.940
She had to be saved by her father while free&nbsp;
falling, they got chased by a massive alien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.860
and she ran around during this action scene in&nbsp;
normal clothes for an unnecessarily long time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:07.740
Why is this the case? Because we wouldn’t have&nbsp;
this totally awesome suit reveal moment. It’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.200
kind of funny that Scott and Cassie have an entire&nbsp;
educational interaction about using the suit while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.240
literally being in the middle of the battlefield.&nbsp;
But luckily Kang’s androids are comically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.000
ineffective. At the end of the scene, MODOK gets&nbsp;
revealed, and writing-wise, it’s really sad that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.580
he’s a constant punching bag for jokes. It really&nbsp;
undercuts the severity of the situation and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.740
really hard to feel a sense of danger when this&nbsp;
comical face is staring at you. Visually I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:39.540
surprised they didn’t try to make the design of&nbsp;
the face more unhuman-like or is more malformed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.000
instead of just enlarging it. It looks ridiculous&nbsp;
and this character design decision is devoid of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.500
logic to me. I understand that it’s supposed to&nbsp;
be a big head, but it feels like their intentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.900
were making MODOK look funny on purpose since the&nbsp;
writing surrounding him constantly degrades him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:00.000
for humor. Like I wish I could unsee this shot of&nbsp;
his ass, and I do not appreciate being visually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.160
assaulted. This is just the visual side of&nbsp;
things and his story later on gets laughably bad.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:10.140
Looking over at the Van Dyne family, their&nbsp;
journey doesn’t really have much impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.060
the overall plot and is mostly the director or&nbsp;
writer playing out their fantasy of making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.620
star wars movie. In Quantumania though, the&nbsp;
things that happen are so bizarre that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.480
are straight-up confounding or cringe-inducing.&nbsp;
Like this random interaction in the desert where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.000
Janet cuts off an alien’s arm which buys her a&nbsp;
ship, this broccoli alien trying to hit on Hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.900
and the controls of this alien ship having&nbsp;
condom joysticks that Hank miraculously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:41.160
knows how to fly. At this point, I assume&nbsp;
the writer doesn’t care about any of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.380
making sense and is batting away the notion&nbsp;
of logic under the guise of comedy. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:51.300
don’t really find this funny in any way and&nbsp;
am constantly in a wtf expression. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.040
there’s this really weird dialogue interaction&nbsp;
that is so awkward that I don’t know how it made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:08.880
it into the final script. I wonder if Michael&nbsp;
Douglas had an existential crisis while reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.460
the script and saw this dialogue paired with him&nbsp;
getting sucked off by two alien fleshlights. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.380
all of these scenes are essentially driven by the&nbsp;
mystery of who this unknown traveler was at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.880
beginning of the movie. And this mystery is the&nbsp;
opposite of intriguing because everyone in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:29.460
audience already knows that it’s Kang and what he&nbsp;
does. Janet is basically teasing this mega threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.800
at the end of every scene and it’s so dumb because&nbsp;
why wouldn’t she just tell Hope and Hank earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.260
Why wait so long? They were literally walking&nbsp;
in an alien forest for a while with not much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:45.660
to do. Also during Janet’s info dump about her&nbsp;
past with Kang, she reveals that the multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:51.120
is real to Hank. Which is again kind of weird&nbsp;
that she would withhold this crazy scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:57.060
revelation from her husband who would love to know&nbsp;
about that. She has all of this reality-altering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.280
scientific information that she can do so much&nbsp;
good with that she for some reason withholds.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.840
Now that Kang is finally revealed in the&nbsp;
narrative, that means we can have a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.280
between him and Scott. There’s nothing wrong with&nbsp;
this scene and I just wanted to point it out as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.380
being one of the few good scenes in the movie. It&nbsp;
feels like whiplash going from all the goofy shit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.300
that was happening to this scene, but hell I’ll&nbsp;
take any ounce of Kang at this rate to move away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:26.460
from the annoying comedy. From here Kang threatens&nbsp;
to kill Cassie if Scott doesn’t shrink down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:31.860
power source for his ship that Janet exploded.&nbsp;
He obviously agrees and this scene is kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.420
interesting, but there are still multiple things&nbsp;
about it that don’t make sense. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.500
Scott is in a possibility field that splits&nbsp;
himself whenever he is met with making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:46.380
decision. This exponentially goes out of control&nbsp;
and in this crowd, a Baskin Robins Scott shows up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:52.740
Which obviously is impossible given that all of&nbsp;
these versions derive from his current self. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.900
the writer is making a joke that completely breaks&nbsp;
the logic of the scene. They then eventually work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:03.180
together and act like a colony of ants. Which&nbsp;
thematically is cool, but at no point is this a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.580
clever solution because it’s completely detached&nbsp;
from basic physics. Finally, this World War Z&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:14.400
stack of zombies falls down and Hope flies in&nbsp;
to rescue Scott just in time, how convenient.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:20.340
Then together all their possible selves combine&nbsp;
back into themselves, which isn’t explained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:25.440
I have no idea why that happened. But they both&nbsp;
shoot shrinking Pym particles into the core and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.840
it shrinks. Now this is immediately inconsistent&nbsp;
to what just happened before because Scott fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:36.240
a Pym particle and it did absolutely nothing to&nbsp;
the surface. But heyyyy, if we fire like twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:42.060
of them at different points of the surface then&nbsp;
surely that would work. Which it somehow does and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:46.620
it’s also convenient that it shrinks down to&nbsp;
the exact size that they needed it to be. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:51.000
they shot it with twenty and it actually worked&nbsp;
this time then wouldn't it have shrunken down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.760
significantly smaller than what it is now? Janet&nbsp;
only enlarged it with 4 pym particles, so the math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.620
doesn’t add up, or the barrier just so happened&nbsp;
to negate the perfect amount of pym particles for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:07.020
the math to check out. Whatever, there’s so many&nbsp;
questions that you have to ignore to enjoy this.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:12.360
After this, Kang gets the power source for his&nbsp;
ship and instead of instantly plugging it in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:17.400
leaving, he decides to give a villain monologue to&nbsp;
Janet. Which is probably one of the most classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.720
villain tropes at this point and is kind of&nbsp;
funny that Marvel movies are still doing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:27.960
31 movies in. Not including the TV shows. During&nbsp;
this monologue, Kang wants to know what Janet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.340
saw when she touched the neural link on his ship.&nbsp;
Which allowed her to see his memories or whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:37.680
However, it seemed pretty clear what she saw in&nbsp;
the past, and basically said she saw him destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:43.200
planets and timelines. But for some reason, Kang&nbsp;
wants to know the specifics I guess? I don’t know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.120
This is obviously happening to stall for time&nbsp;
and Kang also makes a speech to his androids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:53.220
which these are androids I’m pretty sure, and I&nbsp;
don’t understand why he would need to make this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:58.200
speech to them. Or why he would even care about&nbsp;
the rest of the population of the Quantum realm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:03.360
Just freaking leave my dude. Not to mention that&nbsp;
you left Scott and Hope alive to come back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.100
attack you. You’ve literally been waiting for&nbsp;
this moment for years and you’re delaying your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:13.500
own departure. Also, a funny little moment during&nbsp;
Kang and Janet’s interaction was that she said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.060
trillions of people will die due to timelines&nbsp;
as a whole getting deleted. Now I’m pretty sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:22.260
that number would be astoundingly bigger than&nbsp;
trillions given that we're talking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:28.380
universe here. If Earth almost has a population&nbsp;
of 8 billion people then I’m sure we’d be getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:33.300
into numbers higher than the trillions. This was&nbsp;
just one tiny interaction that doesn’t matter.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:39.420
Right after this, Cassie is being escorted back to&nbsp;
her cell, and by some massive oversight from Kang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:44.880
he didn’t take away her Pym particles or even her&nbsp;
suit and she escapes. That would be like taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.860
Iron Man prisoner and leaving him in his suit to&nbsp;
where he could fly away at any moment. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:54.660
this happened in the theater I was shocked by how&nbsp;
lazy the writing was here. It was also funny how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.860
she enlarged one of the androids and this group&nbsp;
of guards coming down the hallway doesn’t see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.480
him I guess? Also, she shrinks down in plain&nbsp;
view of this group of guards walking towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:09.180
her. So to add to the incompetent bad guy army&nbsp;
list, they are blind, have slow reaction times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:14.460
and have terrible aim. They are starting to&nbsp;
sound like something very familiar. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:19.020
since I’m already making this comparison with&nbsp;
Star Wars, Kang and MODOK are basically like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:24.540
Palpatine and Darth Vader. Especially since Kang&nbsp;
can use the force and utilizes energy beams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:31.200
Coincidence? I think not! Then like 5 minutes&nbsp;
later Janet also escapes captivity by herself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:36.960
At this point in the movie, I completely gave&nbsp;
up because if they refuse to put any handcuffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.460
on their prisoners and there’s basically no&nbsp;
ramifications to being caught, then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:46.800
is no danger for these characters. The fact that&nbsp;
the writer did this twice is flat-out insulting.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.620
Hank Pym gets attacked by MODOK while in the&nbsp;
fleshlight ship and it crashlands. Luckily though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:57.300
MODOK doesn’t even bother to check if Hank&nbsp;
is alive and doesn’t confirm the kill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:02.940
Kind of betrays the name of MODOK since his name&nbsp;
stands for Mechanized Organism Designed Only for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:09.420
Killing. So not even checking to see if a target&nbsp;
who he also hates is actually dead is wild. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:13.920
Hank gets out of the crash he finds his colony of&nbsp;
smart ants that got sucked into the Quantum Realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.420
at the start of the movie. It’s revealed here&nbsp;
that the ants went through a time dilation zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:23.520
that made it to where they lived thousands of&nbsp;
years in a single day. They created an advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.220
civilization and are now a thriving society who&nbsp;
seeked out Hank to save him. This is probably the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:34.320
most convenient thing I’ve ever seen in a movie&nbsp;
and now the good guys have an ant army out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:40.140
nowhere. A literal deus ex machina that they try&nbsp;
to downplay as being clever since Hank got random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:44.400
transmissions in his hearing aid or whatever.&nbsp;
But with so much random nonsense happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:49.320
throughout the movie, it’s hard to take anything&nbsp;
as being foreshadowing. Also, these Quantum Realm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:55.080
mechanics are really inconsistent. There’s just&nbsp;
time dilation pockets in random areas. Going back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:59.520
to Cassie, she breaks out the leader of the&nbsp;
rebels and they hatch a plan to hijack Kang’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.200
broadcast to tell everyone to rebel. There’s a&nbsp;
couple minor things here that are questionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:09.180
like Cassie not knowing how to open the cell&nbsp;
and luckily smashes a guard's face into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:14.100
which opens it. Although this is contradicted&nbsp;
five minutes later when the rebel leader inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.500
a code into a panel that unlocks it. Not sure how&nbsp;
she knows that but ok. And the other thing being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:24.540
that they somehow know where the broadcasting&nbsp;
room is in this foreign compound. If I can barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:29.160
navigate Hogwarts with a dedicated map in the&nbsp;
bottom left of my screen then I don’t know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:34.080
they could find a very specific room in this&nbsp;
massive building. That they’ve also never been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:38.700
in before. Unless if there’s just a convenient&nbsp;
map on the wall marking the broadcasting room.

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.960
Kang then sends MODOK after Cassie and&nbsp;
he eventually bests her and is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.780
kill her. But wait, she has plot armor and&nbsp;
MODOK decides not to kill her and instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:53.940
gets distracted by Scott walking around in the&nbsp;
background. Like this is a guaranteed way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:58.740
not make anyone care about your action when&nbsp;
you know there will be no consequences. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:03.600
even crazier thing is that this happens again&nbsp;
and MODOK chooses to talk to Cassie instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:07.920
of killing her. Like I just don’t care&nbsp;
anymore. Then she turns big and wrecks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.480
MODOK. Their following scene together is&nbsp;
probably the worst scene in the entirety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.340
of the MCU. I’m not exaggerating because I&nbsp;
genuinely can’t think of a worse scene than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:29.400
this. Just don’t be a dick 4head.&nbsp;
And then he turns into a good guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:34.500
like WHATTTT!? Him sacrificing himself and&nbsp;
quote on quote becoming an avenger is such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:39.660
a bizarre turn of events. Especially with how&nbsp;
they treat his death scene. It’s so awkward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:44.340
it tries to be funny when it clearly isn’t,&nbsp;
and then the music climaxes as an attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:49.260
punctuate this scene as being a big moment&nbsp;
when it’s not. At this point in the theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.640
when originally watching it I looked over at&nbsp;
my girlfriend in complete shock and we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:58.680
just like what are we even watching right now. I&nbsp;
want to put you into the mind space of Darryn aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:04.800
MODOK. This is a man who hated and was trying&nbsp;
to kill Antman and in the process got banished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:09.240
to the quantum realm. This turned him into a&nbsp;
disfigured monster and the amount of resentment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:15.000
towards Scott for doing this must of have been&nbsp;
unimaginable. Kang then manipulated this man into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:20.820
becoming a weapon and he massacred thousands of&nbsp;
people and even loved doing so. His mental state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:25.380
should be long gone from killing people for&nbsp;
so long and should not be salvageable by any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:31.560
degree. So the fact that he even has this arc, to&nbsp;
begin with, is absurd. I don’t think there is any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.560 --> 00:16:36.960
realistic angle in which you could give MODOK, a&nbsp;
being so filled with rage and revenge for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:42.840
a redemption arc. And it’s even crazier that to&nbsp;
change his mind, a character just tells him not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:49.500
to be a dick. And that is what made him change&nbsp;
his entire motivation. Jesus Christ I can’t.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:56.100
So finally we arrive to some Kang carnage and&nbsp;
through his showcase here, this is probably one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:00.960
the most glaring examples of nerfing your villain&nbsp;
in real time. He starts out completely wiping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:06.420
the floor with these rebels in which if this blue&nbsp;
beam touches them they instantly go poof. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:11.160
when he fights Ant-man and friends if he shoots&nbsp;
them with his beam of disintegration or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:16.020
that I assume like erases them from time,&nbsp;
they just get knocked back. So right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:21.180
it isn’t consistent despite these two events&nbsp;
happening minutes apart. And I don’t believe their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:26.220
suits should be stopping this since they aren’t&nbsp;
made by vibranium or anything. Also, Kang has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:31.440
literal force and doesn’t use it a single time.&nbsp;
Along with being able to fly around on a platform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:36.240
Like couldn’t he force-hold the three of them here&nbsp;
in this one spot instead of giving yet another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:41.760
villain monologue? Then he gets overwhelmed by&nbsp;
Hank’s ant army and Kang doesn’t use his abilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:46.860
logically. When Kang could use any of his&nbsp;
powers like flying away from the ants to safety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:52.020
erasing them all with his energy beam that cuts&nbsp;
through people like paper, or even just force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:56.760
pushing away ants, it’s crazy that he instead&nbsp;
opts to throw up a force shield that isn’t viable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:02.880
to keep up at all times. This is supposed to be an&nbsp;
Avengers-level threat and he lost to motherfucking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:08.460
ants, man. That is by far the biggest buzzkill&nbsp;
I’ve ever seen. I went into this movie hyped to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:14.160
see a menacing and crazy villain who’s extremely&nbsp;
powerful, but all I got was a buffoon who lost to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:19.980
ants. How the hell did this man conquer a galaxy&nbsp;
and best the military strengths of entire worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:25.800
when he can’t even overcome an army of ants who&nbsp;
can only melee him up close. We only got to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:30.660
him fight for like a few minutes and in that&nbsp;
time it was a mess from a writing standpoint.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:36.300
After this battle, Janet opens up a portal from&nbsp;
Kang’s device back to their homeworld. They all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:41.040
get out but just as Scott is about to step in he&nbsp;
chooses to stay behind and stop Kang from getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:46.740
to Earth. The following fight is actually good and&nbsp;
is very visceral. It shows the immense strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:51.840
of Kang and for once this scene actually made&nbsp;
me respect him as a villain. He’s obliterating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:57.540
Scott in hand-to-hand combat and this was really&nbsp;
one of the only things the movie got right. Scott&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:02.760
realizes that he has no chance and corrupts the&nbsp;
power source fueling the portal and Hope comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.620
back to help Scott defeat Kang. Now this was&nbsp;
a pretty powerful ending at the moment because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:12.420
it was as if Scott was destroying his only way&nbsp;
home and his life with his daughter to defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:18.660
the villain. For once, an action has drastic&nbsp;
consequences. But nope, that notion is instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:24.300
disregarded because Cassie Lang is a brainiac and&nbsp;
somehow fixes the portal in like one minute flat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:29.520
This essentially robs the ending from any sort&nbsp;
of impact for me because it erases the weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:34.860
of this major decision to destroy his only way&nbsp;
home. So the heroes live happily ever after.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.460
The council of Kangs does make&nbsp;
me excited for upcoming movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:43.080
but given all the controversy around&nbsp;
Jonathan Majors, I’m not sure what his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:48.000
future in the MCU is going to look like.&nbsp;
So yeah, that was Ant-Man Quantumania.

